  geopolitics: "The de-escalation trade this page ran for a week is now in open conflict with the tape, and the tape won the session. Prices first, all from TradingEconomics this morning. WTI is 93.166 and Brent 105.657, after settling on Thursday at 94.61 and 106.60 - gains of 2.7% and 3.4%, with both contracts up about 5% at their intraday highs. The weekly readings are mixed at -3.03% for WTI and +1.72% for Brent, but the monthly numbers are what the risk premium actually looks like: WTI +13.30% and Brent +21.53%, with gasoline at 3.4835 and +103.61% on the year. UK gas is 188.20 and +15.99% on the month, coal 143.50 and +9.00%. The trigger was specific and physical: Saudi Arabia intercepted six Houthi ballistic missiles aimed at areas including Yanbu, the Red Sea export hub whose loadings were halted by the September 13 drone attack, and an adviser to Iran's Supreme Leader warned against opening a new front in response to a potential US attack. A second escalation is policy rather than military - the US administration has come out in support of a possible ban on diesel exports as diesel prices hit records, with the Energy Secretary canvassing major American refiners on whether they would restrict exports voluntarily, and EU officials arguing it would damage both sides. That tightens the product crack on top of crude. Against all of it sits the most concrete de-escalation development of the war so far: Iran has handed Washington a written road map providing for a regionwide ceasefire of up to 60 days, a phased reopening of the Strait of Hormuz and an end to the American naval blockade imposed on April 13, and the US military says it has now restored between 60% and 70% of pre-war oil flow through the strait - a very different physical baseline from the 38% this page carried a week ago. That road map is what capped Thursday's 5% intraday gain at a 3.4% settle. So the honest position is that the supply risk is escalating and the diplomatic track is advancing at the same time, and USOIL flips from RANGE to a LOW-MED long on the balance rather than on conviction. The FX read has changed in four specific places. First and biggest, Britain: two Bank of England deputy governors who voted to hold eight days ago said on Thursday that persistent energy prices may force them to hike, which is the clearest instance yet of the oil shock converting directly into a policy repricing - November went to roughly 75% and GBP to +1. Second, the euro area: markets now price at least one ECB hike by year-end, and the German ifo at 89.9 says the bloc is absorbing record fuel costs while still accelerating. Third, Canada, where the direction reverses: Macklem's hawkish conditional was written at US$100 oil and Brent is now more than five dollars through it with producer prices at 12.4%, so the CAD forward moves from dovish to balanced even as the score stays at -1 on domestic demand. Fourth, New Zealand, where Governor Breman's oil pass-through warning has taken October pricing to between 75% and 87%. Set against those four, the two central banks that cannot respond - the Bank of Japan, which TE explicitly forecasts to hold at 1.25%, and the SNB, pinned at 0.00% to 2029 - are now the currencies at the bottom of this board, and that is not a coincidence. The one thing that has not changed is the most striking: neither haven is bidding. Gold is -7.25% on the month and the franc is 2.95% weaker against the dollar, both while missiles are being intercepted over a Red Sea export terminal. A market that will not pay for protection into that is pricing an energy-inflation event, not a security event. It is the assumption this entire page rests on, and the Iranian road map breaking down is what breaks it.",

